{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794448351,"nonce":"5K606FNAOPS5q1cI5nrj7Ll3/00=","message":"Direct wasm is now the default for wasm builds, 2026-11-12. Commits 0fab0d77, d897d86e, d6128244 (local, not pushed). Three defects found on the way; two of them are older than this change.\n\n**The default.** `ASH_WASM_DIRECT` unset (or `only`) builds the whole program with no LLVM module; `1` is the mixed build; `0`, `off` or `llvm` is LLVM alone. A program the whole-program build cannot take (a `--pgo` profile, or an embedder's host exports, closures and object hooks) is built by LLVM as before.\n\n**The threads suite caught what the unit suite could not.** With the default flipped, `wasm-fibers` on the Haxe `threads` suite stopped passing (22/22 under LLVM): a trap or a busy hang, depending on the build. The unit suite is single threaded and never suspends a fiber, so it ran 1069/1069 throughout. Bisecting by function (`direct` for a findex range, LLVM for the rest) named `EventLoop.loop` and then `Thread.runWithEventLoop`, and small Haxe functions reproduced both. They are two defects in the fiber transform, not in the emitter; LLVM's output rarely has the shape that exposes them and the direct emitter's has it in almost every function, because it puts the code after a loop inside the loop's last arm.\n\n1. Re-entering an `if` on a rewind needs its condition back. A computed condition is spilled to a pool local, and the pool was sized as \"the most any one boundary asks for\", so every boundary used locals 0..n and two ifs nested on the way to a suspend point shared one. A rewind into the inner arm found the inner condition where the outer one belonged and entered the outer `if` the wrong way round.\n2. A condition that is a bare `local.get` is not spilled at all: the rewind re-reads the local. The direct emitter shares locals by liveness, so the arm can reuse that local before the fiber stops, and the rewind read the new value. The planner now spills such a condition when the arm writes the local.\n\nBoth are fixed in `fiber.rs` and each has a test that suspends inside the arm and checks the rewind: `[1, 100, 3]` without the change, `[1, 2, 3]` with it.\n\n**References in wasm locals were invisible to the collector.** LLVM-built wasm keeps every reference register in the shadow stack on purpose (`pin_register`), because the collector scans that stack and cannot see a wasm local. The direct emitter held them in locals. A reference live across a call, an allocation or a barrier store, or at a loop header, is now also written to a slot in the frame as it is defined, and references have locals of their own so the slots can be told by local. I did not find a program that failed for this reason; it is the same exposure `pin_register` closes, applied to the second backend.\n\n**Gate** (Mac, release `ash`, per-case isolation, no `ASH_WASM_DIRECT`):\n\n    unit, wasm          1069/1069 cases, 10971/10971 assertions\n    unit, wasm-fibers   1069/1069 cases, 10971/10971 assertions\n    threads, wasm-fibers   ALL TESTS OK (the threads suite under plain wasm times out, as it always has)\n\n`cargo test` for `ash_wasm_link`, `ash_wasm_emit`, `wasm_direct_mixed` (12), `wasm_emit_numeric`, `wasm_target`, `wasm_object_roundtrip` and the fiber integration tests pass; clippy is clean on the two crates and `ash_core`.\n\n**Build cost** (Pocket Studio, fibers, Mac): LLVM 15.4 s wall, 46.6 CPU-s, 1135 MB, 17.2 MB module; default 4.6 s wall, 4.3 CPU-s, 225 MB, 14.6 MB module. Bench timings are on the previous comment.\n\nOpen: the fiber `loopSum` in wasmtime (see the previous comment), the unwind exit that keeps every local live (cold compile of the unit suite under wasmtime with fibers), and 7fb763c.","files":null}]}